Subject: The native-stack scan looks up the stack's words, not every parked value's ranges

What this does
  - quickjs.c: `ss_scan` reads the stack first and keeps only the words that can name a heap block (inside the span of addresses the allocator has returned blocks from, which `js_heap_note` maintains at every block allocation). Those words go into a granule hash with a bit filter (a sorted array only for long ranges); each parked value then tests its object, property and element ranges against the filter and looks up only on a hit.

Why we need it
  The scan sorted and searched a range table of every parked value (3,000 or more per reconcile) and looked up every stack word in it: 11% of pdfjs cycles, 27% of raytrace cycles and 40% of splay cycles went to `ss_rng_*`, qsort and the scan itself.

Soundness
  A word inside two ranges pins both values (the earlier nearest-start rule pinned one), which is a superset; a word outside the heap span cannot name a block. Interior pointers into long ranges are found through the sorted array.

Failure modes
  None beyond the earlier scan's conservative pinning.

Tests
  Octane pdfjs, raytrace, splay, deltablue, richards and earleyboyer units run to a score with their checks; the pin phase per scan fell by about half.

